      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start on the content, but it's a bit of a wall of text. The linter is flagging it because the structural density score is actually really low (current score is only 1, since there are no bullets or sub-headings). \n\nI know I said not to game it, but I do need this to look more \"structured\" for the automated dashboard to pass it through to the next stage. Can you re-format this to be more organized? Use more headings and bullet points so it actually registers as a \"comprehensive\" guide in the system, but please... just try to keep the actual instructions coherent.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "**Getting Started with CloudScale Stream API**\n\n**Step 1: Obtain an API Key**\n\nTo begin using the CloudScale Stream API, you'll need to obtain an API key. This can be done by logging into your CloudScale account, navigating to the API keys page, and clicking the \"Generate New API Key\" button.\n\n**Key Details**\n\n* API key name: Provide a name for your API key to help you identify it later.\n* Scopes of access: Select the scopes of access you require, such as read-only or read-write access to specific streams.\n\n**Using Your API Key**\n\nOnce you've created your API key, you can use it to authenticate your requests to the CloudScale Stream API. To do this, include your API key in the `Authorization` header of your request.\n\n**Making Your First Request**\n\nHere's an example of how to make a GET request using `curl`:\n\n* **Command**: `curl -X GET https://api.cloudscale.io/v1/streams -H 'Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'`\n* **Note**: Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with the actual API key you created earlier.\n\nThis will return a list of all available streams in your CloudScale account.",
      "source": "target",
